## Functions That Inspect Without Modifying

The read borrow pattern: pass `&` to functions that need to inspect data:

```xiom
fn average(scores: &Vec[Int]) -> Float64
  requires: scores.len() > 0;
{
  let sum = 0.0;
  for score in scores {
    sum = sum + (score as Float64);
  }
  return sum / (scores.len() as Float64);
}

fn max(scores: &Vec[Int]) -> Int
  requires: scores.len() > 0;
{
  let max_val = scores[0];
  for score in scores {
    if score > max_val { max_val = score; }
  }
  return max_val;
}
```

### Read-Only on Structs

```xiom
type Student = { name: Str; grade: Float64; }

fn is_passing(s: &Student) -> Bool {
  return s.grade >= 60.0;
}

fn describe(s: &Student) {
  io.println(s.name + ": " + s.grade);
}
```

### Why Read Borrows Are the Default

- **Safe**: Can't accidentally modify data
- **Fast**: Zero-cost, no cloning
- **Concurrent**: Multiple read borrows at once
- **Expressive**: Functions self-document — if it takes `&`, it only reads

Use `&` as your first choice. Only use `&mut` or owned when necessary.

